#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a47ce42d-7fbb-5f7c-999d-4c276632310b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"}] :question/id #uuid "5fb13d73-4955-5ae4-bb31-bec3aef3cca9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13237" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n![](images/4e/4eb97ebf3c509832a78f507427a4dead8caf2e495528121f29caf4f1e6b78882.svg)\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n![](images/da/da5c94a138a2a1c37b4eca700822c11d1740bc7f191272bd7b04eb4f0331bf36.svg)\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some ![](images/fb/fb9a89f02c5d1f70a8213f78ab487122536a4488f27a191baddf7cc11e0190ae.svg) we have\n![](images/90/90d5285154002b29f21dc2371e4d8cbe4680e683cc5cef46f0ca8677df1d2510.svg)\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/05/0531b1f1bc71a31d4cd672c8a78690516d9a68c81870bf1f0723d939e239f4d9.svg)\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ![](images/55/55883265eb6b2ec016ed0eb7afadd4e2d2d5183786e048f032d6d6e19f74a843.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n![](images/83/833e64bb77220a52ec87ac4c333439ddf6097151aa992508070af2614a51ae36.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n![](images/0f/0fd03d4f705ce8e2eb6969f528c847d274acd7ccaf816e2510f331ed8544314c.svg)\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k \\geq 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 1},$ we have\n![](images/79/79ca61162feea82659e68725144768b96b947d21c7d0273f0435b07875b3b400.svg)\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/11/11c05d918501f3e52784129b1125fc456d6f61b40f36124ca8666d206fee1c60.svg)\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ![](images/df/dff83649ba6f41c29c1578c82159beff07d1355f4ad730dc4485e5af25de8a93.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6befd731-de3f-545c-aca4-b51b67c68ace" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Matrix Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "d0ade273-b9ef-5c2d-bdcc-3f56f95e79ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-13238" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n![](images/07/07547d9d90fe3939d5ce9a528a2fd37a56980b566d17ee057436b6fcc4694fff.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n![](images/d7/d7e3e5ed2dc60dd8a7a40711798ba589649a7a85cfb01630bdc0e63d779eee8e.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{1}-1 & {4}^{1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n![](images/fc/fc4407495fc6621f5e80daf8846c11206289d4be5fe4a8af9f67721291216a89.svg)\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/7f/7fc2b8a2b2e0ec12f515afcb2d730adf2747ef3cea41f2c97d2d87df9e139118.svg)\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows:\n\n![](images/09/099b3ed6a4c4dd958d1df9dbf507c775cf9a4a9f42ed661e5f8937f70cd46109.svg)\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k}-1 & {4}^{k}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k}-1+3\\cdot {4}^{k} & 4\\cdot {4}^{k}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4\\cdot {4}^{k}-1 & 4\\cdot {4}^{k}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k+1}-1 & {4}^{k+1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n![](images/f6/f67905088dd4d08876f93a46f2997497279c9eaadcdb4cc501ca932cb46265c2.svg)\nis true for all $n\\geq 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a47ce42d-7fbb-5f7c-999d-4c276632310b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ef93ae-39b9-5757-8300-eea0058de3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k+1) & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409734} {:answer/id #uuid "77e5ea63-5fed-50b6-9dcb-f4c2f11bb088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409738} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5fa8b6-63c2-53a6-8dea-a50e0f543c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5\\,\\,k & 1\\end{al