-b273-a72694e43af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5k & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409730} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d09da4-0b4d-5956-9db5-99d9e639a583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k+1) & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d09da4-0b4d-5956-9db5-99d9e639a583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k+1) & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409761} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6289594-4e78-5c29-ae63-3c1f667cff4c" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2d53f1a-2ddc-5a46-80ab-ba5c178f639a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183767" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements." :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(1) & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5\\,\\,k & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k+1) & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a47ce42d-7fbb-5f7c-999d-4c276632310b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292ff5a1-251f-5652-976f-b3a9b466bebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{