b655179-8ac3-57cd-9f13-2108dac06d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "620744c0-c3e0-59b6-a93c-211d1397e447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409735} :answer-field/id #uuid "d81ffe07-0933-57de-af37-f91cd6f9843b" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409783} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a21ef1-ad14-5ba1-b9a9-778954aaff8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409720} {:answer/id #uuid "33628484-8f60-566c-ae05-62ac6120b01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409725} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd12c69-c7af-5046-a9a6-e13853baf0da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13a21ef1-ad14-5ba1-b9a9-778954aaff8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409720} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede3d22e-04a2-5ad1-a82a-03fab92152db" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a379f14-e14d-5731-8273-6d8b7d1a1228" :question/math-academy-id "q-183793" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements." :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{1} & \\frac{{3}^{1}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{k} & \\frac{{3}^{k}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already know that the statement is true for some value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{k+1} & \\frac{{3}^{k+1}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"}]}
